Overall theme: Evidence compares combination immunotherapy (nivolumab + ipilimumab) with targeted BRAF/MEK inhibitor combinations (dabrafenib + trametinib; vemurafenib + cobimetinib), and characterizes efficacy, predictors of long-term benefit, and safety of dabrafenib + trametinib (D+T) in unresectable/metastatic BRAF V600–mutant melanoma.

1) Comparative efficacy: nivolumab + ipilimumab vs BRAF/MEK inhibitors (MAIC)
- Citation: Atkins MB et al. “Comparative efficacy of combination immunotherapy and targeted therapy in the treatment of BRAF-mutant advanced melanoma: a matching-adjusted indirect comparison.” Immunotherapy. 2019. DOI: 10.2217/imt-2018-0208. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30852924/
- Design/Objective: Matching-adjusted indirect comparison assessing clinical outcomes of nivolumab + ipilimumab versus BRAF + MEK inhibitor combinations (dabrafenib + trametinib or vemurafenib + cobimetinib) in patients with BRAF-mutant advanced melanoma.
- Outcomes: Comparative clinical outcomes (e.g., progression-free survival, overall survival, response). Abstract indicates the comparative framework but does not provide specific results here.

2) Predictors of long-term outcomes with dabrafenib + trametinib (3-year pooled analysis)
- Citation: Schadendorf D et al. “Three-year pooled analysis of factors associated with clinical outcomes across dabrafenib and trametinib combination therapy phase 3 randomised trials.” Eur J Cancer. 2017. DOI: 10.1016/j.ejca.2017.05.033. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28648698/
- Context: Builds on prior pooled analysis (median follow-up 20.0 months) showing baseline lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and number of organ sites with metastasis predict progression-free and overall survival with D+T.
- Aim: Longer-term (3-year) pooled follow-up across phase 3 randomized trials to confirm which patients derive maximum durable benefit from D+T, focusing on baseline LDH and metastatic burden as key predictive factors.

3) Review of dabrafenib + trametinib in BRAF V600–mutant advanced melanoma
- Citation: Dhillon S. “Dabrafenib plus Trametinib: a Review in Advanced Melanoma with a BRAF (V600) Mutation.” Target Oncol. 2016. DOI: 10.1007/s11523-016-0443-8. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27246822/
- Indication: D+T (BRAF inhibitor + MEK inhibitor) indicated, as monotherapy or in combination, for unresectable or metastatic melanoma with a BRAF V600 mutation.
- Efficacy (treatment-naïve): In two large phase III trials:
  - COMBI-d (double-blind): D+T significantly prolonged progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS), improved objective response rate (ORR), and preserved health-related quality of life (HR-QoL) more than dabrafenib alone.
  - COMBI-v (open-label): D+T significantly improved PFS, OS, ORR, and HR-QoL versus vemurafenib alone.
- Post–BRAF inhibitor progression: Limited benefit of D+T after progression on prior BRAF inhibitor monotherapy, with ORRs ≤15% and stable disease in ≤50% in small phase I/II studies.
- Safety/tolerability: Combination therapy did not increase overall toxicity relative to dabrafenib or vemurafenib monotherapy. Most adverse events were mild to moderate and manageable. Notably fewer skin-related AEs (e.g., cutaneous malignancies, hyperkeratosis, hand-foot syndrome), likely due to reduced paradoxical MAPK pathway activation with combined BRAF/MEK blockade.
- Conclusion: D+T is an important first-line option for patients with BRAF V600–mutant unresectable or metastatic melanoma, offering superior efficacy and manageable safety versus BRAF inhibitor monotherapy.

Key takeaways across studies:
- Combination immunotherapy (nivolumab + ipilimumab) and targeted therapy combinations are both major options for BRAF-mutant advanced melanoma; indirect comparisons assess relative clinical outcomes.
- With D+T, baseline LDH and number of metastatic organ sites are strong predictors of PFS and 
