Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'KEYNOTE-054 BRAF subgroup recurrence-free survival melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1158 tokens): - Clinical efficacy: EORTC 1325/KEYNOTE-054 (phase III, double-blind) evaluated 12 months of adjuvant pembrolizumab versus placebo in patients with completely resected, high-risk stage III melanoma.
  - Primary findings (earlier analyses): Pembrolizumab significantly prolonged recurrence-free survival (RFS) and distant metastasis-free survival (DMFS) versus placebo.
  - Quantified effect from early analysis: At a median 1.25-year follow-up based on 351 RFS events, pembrolizumab improved RFS (HR 0.57). 
  - Durability: Stability of the RFS and DMFS benefits was previously reported at median follow-ups of 3.5 and 5 years.
  - Seven-year update (2024): The latest publication reports longer follow-up (seven-year analysis) of the same trial population to assess the persistence of benefit over time. The abstract reiterates prior significant RFS and DMFS advantages and notes this extended follow-up; specific updated effect sizes are not provided in the abstract.

- Economic evaluations:
  - Argentina (2021): Cost-effectiveness of adjuvant pembrolizumab versus watchful waiting in stage III melanoma with lymph node involvement after complete resection was evaluated. Context: watchful waiting remains common despite high recurrence risk with surgery alone. Clinical input referenced from KEYNOTE-054 showed improved RFS versus watchful waiting/placebo (HR 0.57; 98.4% CI 0.43–0.74). The abstract describes the evaluation but does not report specific cost-effectiveness results.
  - USA (2020): An economic evaluation compared pembrolizumab with other adjuvant strategies (including novel immune checkpoint inhibitors and targeted therapies) for resected high-risk stage III melanoma from a US health system perspective. Background: pembrolizumab (a PD-1 inhibitor) received FDA approval in 2019 for this indication based on its RFS benefit versus placebo. The abstract outlines objectives and context but does not provide specific cost-effectiveness outcomes.

Key takeaways across sources:
- Pembrolizumab as adjuvant therapy after complete resection in high-risk stage III melanoma consistently improves RFS, with early evidence (HR 0.57) and previously documented stability of benefit at 3.5 and 5 years; a seven-year follow-up update has been reported.
- DMFS is also significantly improved with pembrolizumab versus placebo in this setting (per prior analyses).
- Health economic studies in Argentina and the USA have assessed the value of adjuvant pembrolizumab compared with watchful waiting (Argentina) and other adjuvant options (USA), reflecting evolving standards of care and the therapy’s regulatory approval based on survival benefits; the provided abstracts do not include numerical cost-effectiveness results.
