Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'BRIM-3 final overall survival vemurafenib dacarbazine melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6799 tokens): Here’s a concise synthesis of the key information across the four sources (focus: BRAF inhibitors in metastatic melanoma, related trials, outcomes, safety, health-technology assessment, and the 2015 Melanoma/Immunotherapy Bridge meeting context).

1) Vemurafenib in BRAFV600 mutation-positive metastatic melanoma: BRIM-3 final OS report (Chapman et al., 2017; Ann Oncol; doi:10.1093/annonc/mdx339; PubMed 28961848)
- Context: BRIM-3 previously demonstrated improved progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS) versus dacarbazine in treatment-naive BRAFV600 mutation–positive metastatic melanoma.
- Contribution: Presents the final OS results from BRIM-3. (Abstract reiterates prior PFS/OS benefit and indicates mature survival data were reported; numerical details not provided in this abstract.)

2) Vemurafenib overview and pivotal efficacy/safety (Keating, 2012; Drugs; PubMed 22946753)
- Indication: First-in-class oral inhibitor targeting BRAFV600E (US: BRAFV600E mutation; EU: BRAFV600 mutation) for unresectable or metastatic melanoma.
- BRIM-3 (phase III, previously untreated stage IIIC/IV BRAFV600E+): 
  - OS (co-primary): Risk of death reduced by 63% at interim; by 56%, 38%, and 30% in subsequent updates versus dacarbazine. Most recent median OS: 13.6 months (vemurafenib) vs 9.7 months (dacarbazine).
  - PFS (co-primary): Median PFS 5.3 vs 1.6 months; final analysis showed a 74% reduction in risk of death or progression.
- Phase II (previously treated BRAFV600+ stage IV): Overall response rate 53% (CR 6%, PR 47%); median duration of response 6.7 months; median OS 15.9 months.
- Safety: Generally well tolerated; frequent cutaneous adverse events; cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma occurred in 18% in BRIM-3.

3) Dabrafenib STA (NICE) Evidence Review Group perspective (Fleeman et al., 2015; Pharmacoeconomics; doi:10.1007/s40273-015-0276-9; PubMed 25906420)
- Clinical evidence:
  - BREAK-3 (phase III; n=230; dabrafenib vs dacarbazine; 2:1 randomization): Significant improvement in median PFS with dabrafenib; no significant OS improvement at the time of analysis.
  - Comparator relevance: Vemurafenib (BRIM-3) considered the more appropriate comparator than dacarbazine, given its demonstrated PFS and OS benefits versus dacarbazine.
  - Indirect treatment comparison (dabrafenib vs vemurafenib): Company reported no significant differences in PFS/OS; ERG questioned methodological assumptions and deemed the ITC unreliable.
- Economic evaluation (with Patient Access Scheme [PAS] discounts applied to drug costs):
  - Company base-case ICERs: Dabrafenib vs dacarbazine £60,980/QALY; dabrafenib vs vemurafenib £11,046/QALY.
  - ERG critique: Model overly complex with unsubstantiated assumptions (notably OS projections). Using updated BREAK-3 OS in a simpler model increased dabrafenib vs dacarbazine ICER to £112,727/QALY. Given ITC concerns, ERG considered cross-drug cost-effectiveness unreliable.
- NICE Appraisal Committee conclusion: Despite data limitations, dabrafenib and vemurafenib were “likely” similarly effective clinically; with similar overall costs under PAS, dabrafenib was recommended for NHS England in unresectable, advanced, or metastatic BRAFV600 mutation–positive melanoma.

4) Melanoma and Immunotherapy Bridge 2015 (Naples) proceedings (Nanda et al., 2016; J Transl Med; doi:10.1186/s12967-016-0791-2; PubMed 27461275)
