 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 09:58:11,704 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Executive snapshot (through 2025‑12‑01 UTC)
- Solar flares: A sequence of M‑class flares erupted near/beyond the east limb on 2025‑11‑28–29, followed by an X1.9 flare at 2025‑12‑01 02:49 UTC from N23E90 (the east limb).
- Associated CMEs: Multiple fast CMEs (up to 1,932 km/s) were launched to the east/northeast. The X1.9 flare produced a fast halo CME analyzed at 1,837 km/s.
- Earth impact so far: Evidence points away from geoeffective impacts from these late‑Nov/Dec‑1 events. CMEs were launched from near/beyond the east limb (E70–E90), WSA‑Enlil model runs and DONKI notifications highlight impacts to other spacecraft/planets (e.g., Parker Solar Probe, Mars) but not Earth, and there are no DONKI geomagnetic‑storm (GST) entries for 2025‑11‑27 to 12‑01 even though DONKI GST records are present for earlier November storms.
- Geospace drivers observed instead: Coronal‑hole high‑speed streams (HSS) were logged on 2025‑11‑20, 11‑24, and 11‑28. A radiation‑belt enhancement on 2025‑11‑25 is attributed to the 11‑24 HSS.

What exactly happened (with sources)
Key flares and their linked CMEs (times UTC; speeds are DONKI analyses):
- 2025‑11‑28 22:11 M5.9 (S17E89) → CME start 23:45; 938 km/s, east-directed
  • CME page: DONKI 43090 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43090/-1
- 2025‑11‑29 04:14 M1.5 (S16E89) → CME start 04:00; 854 km/s, east-directed
  • CME page: DONKI 43100 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43100/-1
- 2025‑11‑29 08:40 C7.0 (N20E89) → CME start 09:00; 1,193 km/s, east-directed (triangulated near N05E79)
  • CME page: DONKI 43119 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43119/-1
- 2025‑11‑29 15:36 M2.9 (N20E89) → CME start 16:00; 935 km/s, east-directed
  • CME page: DONKI 43125 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43125/-1
- 2025‑12‑01 02:49 X1.9 (N23E90) → Halo CME start 02:48; 1,837 km/s (On-type analysis)
  • CME page: DONKI 43155 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
  • DONKI flare notifications (X1.9): 20251201‑AL‑001/‑002 (listed in DONKI notifications feed)

Additional fast CMEs on 2025‑11‑29 (with no clear Earth‑facing sources; geometry north/east/far‑side per coronagraph triangulation):
- 05:48 CME; 1,402 km/s (DONKI 43103)
- 06:23 CME; up to 1,932 km/s (DONKI 43108)
- 06:48 CME; 645 km/s (DONKI 43110)
Selected links:
  • 43103 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43103/-1
  • 43108 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43108/-1
  • 43110 https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43110/-1

Directionality and modeling (why these weren’t geoeffective)
- Source longitudes near/beyond E90: Most late‑Nov flares/CMEs came from N/S ~15–25 near E89–E90 (just over the limb). DONKI notes and SUVI diagnostics show limb occultation and far‑sided geometry for several events.
- WSA‑Enlil runs around 2025‑11‑28–30 show impacts at Parker Solar Probe and, for one run, Mars (arrival ~2025‑12‑02T08:00Z), but no Earth impact entries in the model summaries provided:
  • Example: WSA‑ENLIL/43080 (completion 2025‑11‑28 19:08; impacts Parker Solar Probe 2025‑11‑30 01:04; Mars 2025‑12‑02 08:00)
- DONKI notifications corroborate away‑from‑Earth targeting:
  • 2025‑12‑01 CME notification for Mars (ID 20251201‑AL‑003)
  • Multiple 2025‑11‑29–30 CME notifications for Parker Solar Probe
- Geomagnetic storm records: DONKI returns clear GST entries for earlier November storms (e.g., 2025‑11‑12 GST with Kp 8.67–8.33; link: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1), but no GST entries could be retrieved for 2025‑11‑27 to 12‑01 despite multiple retrieval attempts. Given the limb geometry, modeling/notifications, and lack of current GST entries, there is no evidence that the late‑Nov CMEs caused significant geomagnetic storming at Earth as of 2025‑12‑01.

Other geospace drivers observed
- High‑Speed Streams (coronal holes):
  • 2025‑11‑20T13:00Z HSS (DONKI 42930) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/42930/-1
  • 2025‑11‑24T07:23Z HSS (DONKI 42978) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/42978/-1
  • 2025‑11‑28T14:57Z HSS (DONKI 43094) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1
- Radiation belt response:
  • 2025‑11‑25T12:50Z RBE (DONKI 42984) linked to the 2025‑11‑24 HSS https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/RBE/42984/-1

Implications for “this week” (early December)
- Short term (next 3–5 days): The region that produced the X1.9 flare at N23E90 on 2025‑12‑01 will rotate onto the Earth‑facing disk by ~13° per day. Rough guide:
  • ~Dec 3–4: E65–E60
  • ~Dec 5–6: E40–E35
  • ~Dec 7–8: E20–E15
  • ~Dec 10: Near central meridian
  If this region remains flare‑productive, eruptions later this week will have increasingly favorable geometry for Earth‑directed CMEs compared with the events on Nov 28–Dec 1.
- Aurora/geomagnetic activity odds: Based on the data above, the late‑Nov CMEs are unlikely to drive notable geomagnetic storms at Earth. Any near‑term geomagnetic variability is more likely from recurrent HSS. If new CMEs occur as the active region rotates inward, that assessment would change; Earth‑directed CMEs typically arrive ~1–3 days after launch depending on speed.

Caveats and data quality notes
- Coronagraph downlink gaps (e.g., SOHO LASCO C2/C3 on Nov 28–29) and occasional instrument gaps are noted in DONKI entries; DONKI retrieval intermittently failed for some GST/SEP/MPC queries in the Nov 27–Dec 1 window. Nevertheless, available DONKI events, WSA‑Enlil summaries, and notifications consistently indicate non‑Earth‑directed geometry for the key CMEs discussed.

What to monitor (actionable)
- New flares/CMEs from the Dec‑1 X‑flare region as it rotates onto the disk. Look for DONKI CME entries that:
  • have sources east of E60 moving toward central meridian,
  • include Earth in WSA‑Enlil impact lists, and/or
  • trigger DONKI GST/IPS notifications within ~1–3 days after launch.
- HSS recurrence: Keep an eye on daily solar wind and Kp forecasts from NOAA/SWPC. Recurrent HSS from the same coronal holes can continue to modulate geomagnetic conditions even without CMEs.

Primary sources (verifiable)
- DONKI CME (selected):
  • 43155 (2025‑12‑01 halo CME, 1,837 km/s; X1.9 at N23E90) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
  • 43090 (2025‑11‑28 23:45 CME, 938 km/s; M5.9 at S17E89) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43090/-1
  • 43100 (2025‑11‑29 04:00 CME, 854 km/s; M1.5 S16E89) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43100/-1
  • 43119 (2025‑11‑29 09:00 CME, 1,193 km/s; C7.0 N20E89) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43119/-1
  • 43125 (2025‑11‑29 16:00 CME, 935 km/s; M2.9 N20E89) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43125/-1
  • 43103 (2025‑11‑29 05:48 CME, 1,402 km/s) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43103/-1
  • 43108 (2025‑11‑29 06:23 CME, up to 1,932 km/s) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43108/-1
- DONKI flare notifications:
  • X1.9 flare (20251201‑AL‑001, ‑002) listed in DONKI notifications (Dec‑1).
- DONKI HSS/RBE:
  • HSS 42930 (2025‑11‑20) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/42930/-1
  • HSS 42978 (2025‑11‑24) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/42978/-1
  • HSS 43094 (2025‑11‑28) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1
  • RBE 42984 (2025‑11‑25; linked to 11‑24 HSS) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/RBE/42984/-1
- DONKI GST (context for earlier Nov storms):
  • 2025‑11‑12 GST (Kp 8.67–8.33) https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1

Bottom line for your team
- The recent M‑class flares (Nov 28–29) and the Dec‑1 X1.9 flare did produce fast CMEs, but their east‑limb origins and DONKI/WSA‑Enlil products indicate they were not Earth‑directed. As of 2025‑12‑01 there is no DONKI evidence of a geomagnetic storm at Earth attributable to these eruptions.
- Watch the same active region later this week as it rotates onto the disk; if it remains active, subsequent flares could launch Earth‑directed CMEs with storm potential 1–3 days after launch.
- In the interim, geomagnetic variations are more likely to be driven by coronal‑hole high‑speed streams than by the late‑Nov CMEs.
