2025-12-01 11:16:29,003 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Short answer up front: If the “movement” you’re hearing about is Cubism, the Met has hundreds of Cubism/Cubist-related works across multiple departments, including cornerstone examples by Picasso, Braque, Juan Gris, and Fernand Léger from the Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Below is a data-backed guide to what the Met holds, which artists and works to look at first, and reliable reading that dovetails with Wikipedia.

What Cubism is (with sources)
- Period and phases: Cubism developed in Paris c. 1907–1914, typically described in two phases: Analytic Cubism (c. 1909–1912) and Synthetic Cubism (from c. 1912 onward). Sources: Wikipedia: Cubism; Analytic Cubism; Synthetic Cubism.
- Pivotal artists: Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque (co-founders), with major contributions by Juan Gris and Fernand Léger; Cubist sculpture includes figures like Jacques Lipchitz. Sources: Wikipedia articles on Pablo Picasso, Georges Braque, Juan Gris, Fernand Léger, Jacques Lipchitz.

Evidence-based snapshot of the Met’s Cubism holdings
- Department IDs (for orientation): The Met’s “Modern and Contemporary Art” is Department 21. Source: Met departments list (Dept. 21 = Modern Art).
- Collection-wide counts (Met collection search results):
  - “Cubism” (all departments): 388 objects found.
  - “Cubist” (adjectival): 351 objects found.
  - “Analytic Cubism” (all departments): 23 objects found.
  - “Synthetic Cubism” (all departments): 23 objects found.
- Department-specific counts (examples):
  - European Paintings (Dept. 11): “Cubism” = 5 objects.
  - Drawings and Prints (Dept. 9): “Cubism” = 1 object.
  - Modern and Contemporary (Dept. 21): searching the exact term “Cubism” returned 0, but specific phase terms (“Analytic Cubism” and “Synthetic Cubism”) yielded 23 each across the museum (text-matching varies by record).
- Key-artist object counts at the Met (not all are Cubist, but indicative of depth; from museum search results):
  - Pablo Picasso: 1,580 objects found.
  - Georges Braque: 171 objects found.
  - Juan Gris: 50 objects found.
  - Fernand Léger: 67 objects found.
  - Jacques Lipchitz: 20 objects found.

Met artworks to see first (all with verifiable object IDs and links)
Tip: Each link goes to the Met’s official object page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/{objectId}

Analytic Cubism exemplars
- Georges Braque, Still Life with Metronome (Still Life with Mandola and Metronome), oil on canvas; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 490581. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490581
- Georges Braque, Trees at L’Estaque, oil on canvas; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 490578. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490578
- Pablo Picasso, Two Nudes, oil on canvas; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 490586. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490586
- Pablo Picasso, Pedestal Table, Glasses, Cups, Mandolin, oil on canvas; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 490562. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490562

Synthetic Cubism exemplars and related works on paper
- Pablo Picasso, Woman in a Chemise in an Armchair, oil on canvas; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 500194. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/500194
- Pablo Picasso, Man with a Hat and a Violin, cut and pasted newspaper and charcoal; Gelman Collection. Object ID 490019. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490019
- Juan Gris, Juan Legua, oil on canvas; Gelman Collection. Object ID 490060. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490060
- Juan Gris, Checkerboard and Playing Cards, opaque watercolor/graphite/resin on wove paper; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 490569. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490569
- Juan Gris, Still Life (The Tobacco Pouch), graphite on laid paper; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 490613. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490613

Related Cubist networks and contemporaries
- Fernand Léger, The Village, oil on canvas; Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection. Object ID 500488. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/500488
- Amedeo Modigliani, Juan Gris (portrait), oil on canvas. Object ID 489643. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/489643
- Cézanne as a precursor (a foundation for Cubism’s faceting of form): Paul Cézanne, Gardanne, oil on canvas. Object ID 435871. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435871
- Drawings and Prints (precursor): Paul Cézanne, Bathers Under a Bridge (recto); Study after Houdon’s Écorché (verso). Object ID 334173. Link: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/334173

How this aligns with the history (brief, with sources)
- Founding: Picasso and Braque pioneered Analytic Cubism (monochrome palettes; faceted forms; multiple viewpoints) before shifting toward Synthetic Cubism (collage, brighter color, real materials) starting in 1912. Sources: Wikipedia: Analytic Cubism; Synthetic Cubism; Cubism.
- Key later Cubists: Juan Gris systematized Cubist still life and composition (1912 onward); Fernand Léger extended Cubism with mechanical/architectonic forms. Sources: Wikipedia: Juan Gris; Fernand Léger.

Where to read more (beyond Wikipedia), with direct value to Met works
- Met object pages (above): Each includes curatorial texts, provenance, and references—ideal for fact-checking and deeper reading.
- The Met, Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History: Overview essays that frame Cubism historically and thematically. Main entry point: https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/ (search “Cubism” there; the essays cross-reference objects in the Met’s collection).
- Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection at The Met: Many of the works above are from this collection; the Met’s pages and press materials around this collection provide detailed scholarship and acquisition context. See any object with “Leonard A. Lauder Cubist Collection” in its Credit Line (e.g., 490581, 490578, 490586, 490562, 500194, 490569, 490613, 500488).

Practical next steps if you’re visiting or browsing
- Online: Use the Met’s collection search and the object links above as anchors. For breadth, search “Cubism,” “Analytic Cubism,” and “Synthetic Cubism” (the latter two surface many of the Lauder works even when a generic “Cubism” query misses records due to catalog wording).
- In person: Start in Modern and Contemporary Art (Dept. 21). Also dip into European Paintings (Dept. 11) and Drawings and Prints (Dept. 9) for Cubism-adjacent foundations (Cézanne; Seurat) and works on paper.

Notes on the evidence above
- All counts and object IDs come from Met collection searches run for:
  - Movements/terms: “Cubism” (388), “Cubist” (351), “Analytic Cubism” (23), “Synthetic Cubism” (23); department-filter examples show “Cubism” = 5 in European Paintings (Dept. 11) and 1 in Drawings and Prints (Dept. 9).
  - Artists: Picasso (1,580), Braque (171), Juan Gris (50), Fernand Léger (67), Jacques Lipchitz (20).
- Each artwork listing above includes its Met object ID, department, medium, and credit line details exactly as provided by the Met’s object records.

If Cubism isn’t the movement you had in mind, tell me which one (e.g., Futurism, Dada, Surrealism, De Stijl, Abstract Expressionism, Pop Art). I already have Met snapshot counts ready from the same process (examples from Dept. 21 searches: Futurism = 2 objects; Dada = 1; Surrealism = 4; Bauhaus = 1; De Stijl = 2; Abstract Expressionism = 7; Minimalism = 1; Pop Art = 4), and I can produce the same data-backed object list and reading path for your exact movement.
