 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 11:49:09,877 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concise, evidence-backed field brief you can drop into a deck or memo. It covers: (1) which transformer LLMs are leading right now, (2) the datasets researchers actually use (pretraining, instruction, and RLHF), (3) the most relevant benchmarks, and (4) recent technical advances with citations and concrete numbers.

1) Practical “frontier” open models for text generation (2024–2025)
- Meta Llama 3.x
  - Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct: 418,468+ downloads, 2,579+ likes on Hugging Face; multi-lingual chat, strong generalist baseline. Model card: meta-llama/Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct.
  - Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct: 695,277+ downloads. Model card: meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct.
  - Paper lineage: LLaMA family (arXiv:2302.13971) emphasizes training with public data at scale.
- Qwen2.5 (Alibaba)
  - Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct: 7,703,231+ downloads; Apache-2.0 license. Model card: Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct.
  - Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct: 533,443+ downloads. Model card: Qwen/Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct.
  - Technical report (Qwen2, arXiv:2407.10671) reports strong scores (e.g., MMLU 84.2, GSM8K 89.5, HumanEval 64.6 for the 72B base).
- Google Gemma 3 (text + “any-to-any” multimodal)
  - Gemma-3-27B-it: 1,208,025+ downloads; Gemma-3-12B-it: 1,448,732+; Gemma-3-4B-it: 939,327+. Model cards: google/gemma-3-27b-it, -12b-it, -4b-it.
  - Tags show broad benchmark coverage and multilingual/translation emphasis (e.g., WMT24++ arXiv:2502.12404).
- Mistral / Mixtral (Mixture-of-Experts)
  - Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1: 382,528+ downloads; 4,612+ likes (Apache-2.0). Model card: mistralai/Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1.
  - Mistral-7B Instruct family: multi-million downloads; efficient GQA/SWA architecture (paper: arXiv:2310.06825).
- Microsoft Phi-3.x (small, on-device capable)
  - Phi-3.5-mini-instruct: 319,035+ downloads; MIT license. Model card: microsoft/Phi-3.5-mini-instruct.
  - Reports: technical (arXiv:2404.14219) and safety post-training (arXiv:2407.13833). Emphasis on small models trained on trillions of tokens.
- DeepSeek
  - DeepSeek-R1 (reasoning, RL): 1,184,480+ downloads; 12,887+ likes. Model card: deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1; paper: “DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning…” (arXiv:2501.12948).
  - Distill variants (e.g., DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B): 482,677+ downloads.

Why these are “frontier”
- Broad adoption: multi-hundred-thousand to multi-million downloads (HF model cards).
- Strong reported benchmarks: Qwen2 (arXiv:2407.10671) publishes top-tier MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval for open-weight models; Mistral shows efficiency/perf via GQA/SWA (arXiv:2310.06825); DeepSeek-R1 highlights RL-boosted reasoning (arXiv:2501.12948).
- Coverage: dense and MoE, long-context variants, small-but-capable on-device options, and multimodal routes.

2) Datasets researchers actually use (with scale)
Pretraining corpora (open, large-scale)
- FineWeb (HuggingFaceFW/fineweb): 15T+ tokens (now ~18.5T reported on HF dataset page), Common Crawl–based, quality-filtered, large dedup; paper: The FineWeb Datasets (arXiv:2406.17557). Finding: its “FineWeb-Edu” 1.3T token subset boosts reasoning/knowledge benchmarks (MMLU, ARC).
- Dolma (allenai/dolma): ~3T tokens (English), diverse sources + full curation toolkit; paper: arXiv:2402.00159.
- RefinedWeb (tiiuae/falcon-refinedweb): 600B-token public extract; web-only but competitive vs curated corpora; paper: arXiv:2306.01116.
- C4 (allenai/c4) from the T5 paper (arXiv:1910.10683): cleaned Common Crawl; foundational to modern text-to-text frameworks.
- RedPajama-Data-1T (togethercomputer/RedPajama-Data-1T) and SlimPajama-627B (cerebras/SlimPajama-627B): open, “clean-room” reproductions/derivatives of “LLaMA-like” mixes.
- The Pile (EleutherAI/the_pile_deduplicated; paper: arXiv:2101.00027): 825GB/22 sources; landmark diverse corpus.

Instruction-tuning / chat datasets (post-training SFT)
- OpenAssistant (OpenAssistant/oasst1): 161k+ messages with 461k+ quality ratings (Apache-2.0; arXiv:2304.07327).
- UltraChat 200k (HuggingFaceH4/ultrachat_200k): filtered synthetic dialogues; used to train Zephyr-7B-β (arXiv:2305.14233).
- Alpaca (tatsu-lab/alpaca; 52k instances) and Alpaca-Cleaned (yahma/alpaca-cleaned): widely used for initial instruction-following.

Preference/RLHF and reasoning-feedback datasets
- Anthropic HH-RLHF (Anthropic/hh-rlhf): human preference data (MIT; arXiv:2204.05862).
- UltraFeedback (openbmb/UltraFeedback; arXiv:2310.01377): high-quality feedback for preference optimization/DPO pipelines.
- Open-Orca / SlimOrca (Open-Orca/OpenOrca; SlimOrca-Dedup): GPT-4–style instruction following (arXiv:2306.02707).

Code corpora for text-to-code generation
- The Stack (bigcode/the-stack): multi-language permissive-licensed GitHub code (arXiv:2211.15533; HumanEval arXiv:2107.03374; also StarCoder line).

3) Benchmarks that matter (with references)
- General knowledge and reasoning
  - MMLU (arXiv:2009.03300): 57 academic/pro domains; widely reported (e.g., Qwen2 paper).
  - MMLU-Pro (TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro; arXiv:2406.01574): harder, more robust successor used in 2024–2025 reporting.
  - TruthfulQA (arXiv:2109.07958): measures susceptibility to imitative falsehoods.
  - HellaSwag (arXiv:1905.07830): commonsense inference at scale.
  - GPQA (arXiv:2311.12022): graduate-level, hard science QA.
- Math and step-by-step reasoning
  - GSM8K (arXiv:2110.14168): grade-school multi-step arithmetic; verification-centric training often helps.
  - BIG-Bench Hard (BBH; arXiv:2210.09261): long-tail compositional reasoning tasks.
- Code generation
  - HumanEval (arXiv:2107.03374) and MBPP (arXiv:2108.07732): pass@k metrics, widely standardized.
- Long-context
  - LongBench (arXiv:2308.14508): multi-task, long-document understanding.

4) What’s actually new in methods (you can cite these directly)
- Data curation at trillion-scale correlates with reasoning gains
  - FineWeb (arXiv:2406.17557): 15T+ tokens with rigorous filtering/dedup; FineWeb-Edu (1.3T tokens) measurably improves MMLU/ARC.
  - Dolma (arXiv:2402.00159): 3T tokens + open curation toolkit enabling ablations on dedup/filtering/mixes.
  - RefinedWeb (arXiv:2306.01116): shows web-only data, if well-filtered, can rival curated mixes.
- Efficient architectures and long-context
  - Mistral 7B (arXiv:2310.06825): grouped-query attention (GQA) + sliding-window attention (SWA) for speed and scalable context.
  - YaRN (arXiv:2309.00071): efficient RoPE-based context extension with far fewer tokens/steps; widely adopted in long-context variants (e.g., Qwen families).
  - Mixture-of-Experts (Mixtral-8x7B): route-of-experts for higher effective capacity at lower per-token FLOPs (evidenced by adoption/popularity on HF).
- Reasoning via reinforcement learning and distillation
  - DeepSeek-R1 (arXiv:2501.12948): uses RL to incentivize chain-of-thought/verification-style reasoning; widely distilled into smaller backbones (e.g., DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B).
- Inference acceleration for text generation
  - Speculative decoding (arXiv:2211.17192): 2–3x faster decoding on T5-XXL with identical outputs via a draft model + verification.
  - These techniques are now mainstream in serving stacks alongside caching, KV-quantization, and paged attention (vendor docs vary; speculative decoding is the most clearly citable academic reference).

5) How to present “who’s leading” with evidence
- Cite model families and HF adoption:
  - Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (7.7M+ downloads), Llama-3.x-70B-Instruct (695k–418k+), Mixtral-8x7B (382k+), Phi-3.5-mini (319k+), Gemma-3 variants (0.9–1.4M+). Model cards listed above.
- Cite published scores where available:
  - Qwen2 technical report (arXiv:2407.10671) for MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval headline numbers.
- Cite data papers for “why these models improved”:
