Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Llama 3 OR Llama 3.1 OR Llama 3.3 arXiv 2024 OR 2025 Meta', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4636 tokens): Here’s a concise, information-preserving summary of the provided papers:

1) Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (cs.CV, 2025-11-28)
- Problem: Video reasoning traces in MLLMs are often logically inconsistent and weakly grounded in visual evidence.
- Diagnostics: Proposes Think Answer Consistency (TAC) for reasoning–answer alignment and Video Attention Score (VAS) for visual vs textual grounding; finds heavy reliance on linguistic priors across 11 video benchmarks.
- Method: Timestamp-aware SFT + GRPO with a Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to improve temporal precision and coherence.
- Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks.
- Availability: Code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.

2) Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV, 2025-11-28)
- Problem: Most MLLMs “think about videos” passively (text-only reasoning after encoding), limiting fine-grained spatio-temporal understanding.
- Method: Interactive Video Reasoning with a Chain of Manipulations (iterative visual actions to gather/verify evidence). Trains on Video-CoM Instruct (18K) and RL with reasoning-aware GRPO using step-level reasoning rewards.
- Results: +3.6% average over recent SOTA on 9 video benchmarks using only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO samples; improved accuracy and interpretability in ablations.
- Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

3) Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI, 2025-11-28)
- Problem: Rigid multi-turn reasoning limits active learning for world model reasoning.
- Method (WMAct): (1) Reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy; (2) Interaction frequency annealing to force condensation of learning and internalization of dynamics.
- Results: On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, solves tasks in a single turn that previously required multiple interactions; strong transfer to complex environments and better performance on reasoning benchmarks.

4) AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV, 2025-11-28)
- Problem: High data cost and difficulty in coherent multi-person audio-driven talking video generation.
- Method: Extensible multi-stream architecture with identity-aware attention in a Diffusion Transformer to iteratively process identity–audio pairs; trains largely on single-person videos, refining interactivity with a small set of real multi-person clips.
- Contributions: Introduces a metric and dataset to evaluate naturalness and interactivity.
- Results: Strong lip sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity with favorable data efficiency and identity scalability.

5) A Lyapunov-Based Small-Gain Theorem for Fixed-Time Stability (eess.SY, 2025-11-28)
- Problem: Lack of small-gain tools for fixed-time stability (FxTS) in interconnected systems.
- Method: If each subsystem has a fixed-time ISS Lyapunov function (FxT-ISS) and a nonlinear small-gain condition holds, the overall interconnection is FxTS.
- Significance: Complements existing small-gain theorems for asymptotic/finite-time stability; fills a theoretical gap.
- Examples: Analytical and numerical demonstrations, including fixed-time feedback optimization without plant–controller time-scale separation.

6) ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (cs.LG; cs.CL, 2025-11-28)
- Problem: AlphaEvolve is closed-source, ensemble-based, and purely inference-time, preventing strategy internalization.
- Method: Open-source framework enabling in-context and RL at test-time with a single LLM; uses a large program database, batch sampling, lazy penalties, and optional reward shaping to stabilize/scale evolving.
- Results: Small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new best-known bounds on circle packing and the first auto-correlation inequality; across 2 models/4 tasks, RL at test-time outperforms inference-only; RL checkpoints show f
Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Qwen2.5 arXiv 2024 OR 2025', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4636 tokens): Here’s a concise, information-preserving summary of the provided papers:

- Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (arXiv:2511.23478v1; cs.CV)
  - Problem: MLLMs often produce convincing but logically inconsistent or weakly grounded video reasoning, relying on linguistic priors.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) to measure alignment between reasoning and answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to assess reliance on visual vs textual cues.
  - Method: Timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to improve temporal precision and causal coherence.
  - Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across 11 video reasoning benchmarks; code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.
  - Links: http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23478v1; PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23478v1

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (arXiv:2511.23477v1; cs.CV)
  - Problem: Most MLLMs “think about” videos in text after a single encoding, limiting fine-grained spatiotemporal reasoning.
  - Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—“think with videos” by iteratively manipulating visuals to gather/verify evidence.
  - Model/Data: Video-CoM uses a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) and Video-CoM Instruct (18K instruction-tuning samples).
  - Training: Supervised tuning + RL using reasoning-aware GRPO with step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards).
  - Results: +3.6% average improvement over recent SOTA across 9 benchmarks with only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO videos; improved accuracy and interpretability in ablations.
  - Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM
  - Links: http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23477v1; PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23477v1

- Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (arXiv:2511.23476v1; cs.AI)
  - Goal: Enable robust, efficient world-model reasoning via active interaction rather than rigid reasoning templates.
  - WMAct: Two mechanisms—(1) reward rescaling (based on action efficacy) to reduce redundancy and promote purposeful interaction; (2) interaction frequency annealing to progressively limit turns, forcing internalization of dynamics.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, solves tasks in a single turn that previously needed multiple; strong transfer and improved reasoning benchmarks.
  - Links: http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23476v1; PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23476v1

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (arXiv:2511.23475v1; cs.CV)
  - Challenge: Multi-person, audio-driven talking video generation with coherent interactivity and low data cost.
  - Method: Extensible multi-stream architecture with identity-aware attention in a Diffusion Transformer to process identity–audio pairs iteratively, enabling arbitrary identity scaling.
  - Training: Leverages only single-person videos to learn multi-person patterns; refines interactivity with few multi-person clips.
  - Contributions: New metric and dataset to evaluate naturalness/interactivity.
  - Results: Strong lip-sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity with favorable data cost–scalability trade-off.
  - Links: http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23475v1; PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23475v1

- A Lyapunov-Based Small-Gain Theorem for Fixed-Time Stability (arXiv:2511.23474v1; eess.SY)
  - Contribution: New Lyapunov-based small-gain framework for fixed-time stability (FxTS) in interconnected systems.
