Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2406.17557'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 174293 tokens): Title: The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at Scale
URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2406.17557
Authors: Guilherme Penedo, Hynek Kydlíček, Loubna Ben Allal, Anton Lozhkov, Margaret Mitchell, Colin Raffel, Leandro von Werra, Thomas Wolf

What’s introduced
- FineWeb: a large-scale, high-quality web text dataset with 15 trillion tokens, built from 96 Common Crawl snapshots.
- FineWeb-Edu: a 1.3-trillion token subset focused on educational text filtered from FineWeb.

Key findings and contributions
- Performance: Models pretrained on FineWeb outperform those trained on prior open web-scale datasets.
- Educational focus boosts reasoning/knowledge: Pretraining on FineWeb-Edu yields substantial gains on knowledge- and reasoning-heavy benchmarks (e.g., MMLU, ARC).
- Transparent curation: The work extensively documents and ablates data curation choices, including:
  - Deduplication strategies: in-depth analysis of methods and their impact.
  - Filtering strategies: systematic study of how different filters affect quality and downstream performance.
- Open resources: Public release of the data curation codebase and all models trained during ablation experiments to advance reproducibility and best practices in dataset construction.

Scale and sources
- 15T tokens total (FineWeb) from 96 Common Crawl snapshots.
- 1.3T tokens (FineWeb-Edu) selected as educational content via targeted filtering.

Why it matters
- Training data quality and scale are central to LLM performance, yet leading open LLMs (e.g., Llama 3, Mixtral) do not disclose detailed pretraining corpora. FineWeb provides a large, well-documented, open alternative with empirical evidence on curation choices.
- FineWeb-Edu demonstrates that targeted, education-focused data can markedly improve reasoning/knowledge benchmarks.

Public releases
- Datasets: FineWeb (15T tokens) and FineWeb-Edu (1.3T tokens).
- Code: Full data curation pipeline.
- Models: All ablation-trained models used to study deduplication and filtering.

Notable implementations referencing or built with FineWeb/FineWeb-Edu (selection)
- BSC-LT models:
  - BSC-LT/ALIA-40b
  - BSC-LT/salamandra-7b and BSC-LT/salamandra-7b-instruct
  - BSC-LT/salamandra-2b and BSC-LT/salamandra-2b-instruct
- NVIDIA Nemocurator classifiers for FineWeb-Edu selection:
  - nvidia/nemocurator-fineweb-nemotron-4-edu-classifier
  - nvidia/nemocurator-fineweb-mixtral-edu-classifier
- Axiomlab Cable models (length-extrapolation/positional encoding research) trained on or referencing FineWeb-Edu or Wiki:
  - axiomlaborg/Cable
  - axiomlaborg/cable-wiki-tiny-512, axiomlaborg/cable-wiki-tiny-1024
  - axiomlaborg/cable-edufineweb-md-512, axiomlaborg/cable-edufineweb-md-1024
- Community/quantized ports and small experiments referencing FineWeb/FineWeb-Edu:
  - fraserlove/gpt-alpha
  - robbiemu/salamandra-2b, robbiemu/salamandra-2b-instruct
  - RichardErkhov/BSC-LT_-_salamandra-7b, RichardErkhov/BSC-LT_-_salamandra-7b-instruct-gguf

In summary
FineWeb is a 15T-token, openly described web pretraining dataset that improves LLM performance relative to other open corpora, with rigorous ablations on deduplication and filtering. FineWeb-Edu, a 1.3T-token educational subset, significantly boosts results on reasoning/knowledge benchmarks like MMLU and ARC. The authors release the datasets, full curation code, and all ablation models, and the ecosystem already includes multiple third-party models and classifiers leveraging these datasets.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '1910.10683'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 135590 tokens): Title: Exploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified Text-to-Text Transformer (T5)

Authors: Colin Raffel, Noam Shazeer, Adam Roberts, Katherine Lee, Sharan Narang, Michael Matena, Yanqi Zhou, Wei Li, Peter J. Liu

Core idea and contributions:
- Proposes a unified text-to-text framework that formulates all NLP tasks (e.g., summarization, question answering, text classification) as text-to-text problems.
- Conducts a systematic study of transfer learning in NLP, comparing pre-training objectives, architectures, unlabeled datasets, transfer approaches, and other factors across dozens of tasks.
- Combines these insights with scale and the new Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus (C4) to achieve state-of-the-art results on many benchmarks.
- Releases the C4 dataset, pre-trained models, and code to support reproducibility and future research.

Key outcomes:
- Unified text-to-text formulation simplifies applying a single model and training objective across diverse NLP tasks.
- Empirical analysis demonstrates the impact of design choices (pre-training objective, architecture, data, transfer method) on downstream performance.
- State-of-the-art performance reported on multiple benchmarks spanning summarization, QA, and classification.

Resources released:
- Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus (C4) for large-scale pre-training.
- Pre-trained models and accompanying code.

Ecosystem and notable implementations referencing this work (selection):
- Google T5 v1.1 family: t5-v1_1-small, base, large, xl, xxl (text2text-generation; trained on C4).
- LongT5 variants: long-t5-local-base, long-t5-tglobal-base/xl (long-context text-to-text).
- Fine-tuned T5 models for specific tasks:
  - Question generation and QA: valhalla/t5-base-e2e-qg, valhalla/t5-base-qa-qg-hl, mrm8488/t5-base-finetuned-question-generation-ap.
  - Summarization: mrm8488/t5-base-finetuned-summarize-news.
  - Emotion/sentiment/sarcasm: mrm8488/t5-base-finetuned-emotion, mrm8488/t5-base-finetuned-sarcasm-twitter.
  - SQL generation: mrm8488/t5-base-finetuned-wikiSQL.
  - Multilingual/other domains: megagonlabs/t5-base-japanese-web.
  - Instruction-tuned: allenai/tk-instruct-11b-def.
  - Encoder-only conversion: mcmonkey/google_t5-v1_1-xxl_encoderonly.
- Time-series forecasting with T5 backbones (Chronos):
  - amazon/chronos-t5 family: tiny, small, base, large, mini.
  - Chronos-Bolt family: amazon/chronos-bolt-tiny/small/base and AutoGluon variants.
- Additional community fine-tunes and applications appear across text generation and domain-specific tasks.

Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/1910.10683

One-sentence summary:
The T5 paper unifies NLP tasks into a single text-to-text framework, rigorously studies transfer learning design choices, introduces the large-scale C4 dataset, and achieves state-of-the-art results across diverse benchmarks, catalyzing a broad ecosystem of T5-based models and applications.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2402.00159'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 30267 tokens): Title: Dolma: an Open Corpus of Three Trillion Tokens for Language Model Pretraining Research

Core idea and motivation:
- Modern LMs rarely disclose or release their pretraining data, hindering research on how data shapes model capabilities and limitations.
- Dolma addresses this by releasing a large, openly available pretraining corpus and the full curation toolkit to enable reproducibility and systematic data studies.

What is Dolma:
- An English pretraining corpus of ~3 trillion tokens.
- Built from a diverse mixture of sources: web content, scientific papers, code, public-domain books, social media, and encyclopedic materials.
- Accompanied by an open-source data curation toolkit to reproduce and extend the dataset and experiments.

Documentation and methodology:
- The report details design principles, construction process, and a summary of corpus contents.
- It interleaves analyses and experiments using intermediate Dolma states to study key data practices:
  - Content/quality filtering
  - Deduplication
  - Multi-source data mixing
- Findings demonstrate how these curation choices impact downstream model performance.

Impact and usage:
- Dolma underpins OLMo, a state-of-the-art open language model and research framework for studying the science of language modeling.
- By making both the corpus and curation pipeline public, the work enables open, reproducible research on data’s role in LM training.

Notable contributors (selection):
- Luca Soldaini, Rodney Kinney, Akshita Bhagia, Dustin Schwenk, David Atkinson, Russell Authur, Ben Bogin, Khyathi Raghavi Chandu, Yanai Elazar, Valentin Hofmann, Ananya Harsh Jha, Sachin Kumar, Li Lucy, Xinxi Lyu, Nathan Lambert, Ian Magnusson, Jacob Morrison, Niklas Muennighoff, Aakanksha Naik, Crystal Nam, Matthew E. Peters, Abhilasha Ravichander, Kyle Richardson, Zejiang Shen, Emma Strubell, Nishant Subramani, Oyvind Tafjord, Pete Walsh, Luke Zettlemoyer, Noah A. Smith, Hannaneh Hajishirzi, Iz Beltagy, Dirk Groeneveld, Jesse Dodge, Kyle Lo, and others.

Implementations/resources:
- The Hugging Face page hosts numerous AllenAI “DataDecide” OLMo checkpoints exploring data-mix and curation choices (e.g., dolma1_7 variants, C4 baselines, FineWeb-edu/pro subsets, with tokens ranging from a few million to ~1B), facilitating comparison of filtering, deduplication, and source mixing. Models are generally:
  - English, hf_olmo format, Apache-2.0 licensed
  - Tagged with relevant arXiv references and dataset provenance
  - Examples include: allenai/DataDecide-dolma1_7-90M, -no-reddit-1B, -no-math-code-750M; -c4-16M/750M; -fineweb-edu-10M/14M; -fineweb-pro-8M; and mixed-source baselines.

Link:
- https://huggingface.co/papers/2402.00159
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2306.01116'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 335092 tokens): Title: The RefinedWeb Dataset for Falcon LLM: Outperforming Curated Corpora with Web Data, and Web Data Only

Authors: Guilherme Penedo, Quentin Malartic, Daniel Hesslow, Ruxandra Cojocaru, Alessandro Cappelli, Hamza Alobeidli, Baptiste Pannier, Ebtesam Almazrouei, Julien Launay

Core idea and contributions:
- Challenge to prevailing belief: High-performing LLMs do not require curated corpora (books, papers, social media) if web data is properly filtered and deduplicated.
- Data scaling: From CommonCrawl alone, the team extracted 5 trillion tokens of high-quality web text with extensive filtering and deduplication.
- Public release: A 600B-token extract of the RefinedWeb dataset, plus language models trained on it with 1.3B and 7.5B parameters.
- Performance: Models trained on RefinedWeb significantly outperform state-of-the-art models trained on The Pile, demonstrating that filtered/deduplicated web-only data is sufficient and scalable for strong zero-shot generalization.

Paper URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2306.01116

Key dataset details:
- Name: RefinedWeb (derived from CommonCrawl).
- Scale: 5T tokens (internal); 600B-token public extract.
- Method: Heavy filtering and deduplication to obtain high-quality web-only data.
- Motivation: Scalable alternative to curated datasets as models push into trillions of tokens.

