Authors: Leo Gao, Stella Biderman, Sid Black, Laurence Golding, Travis Hoppe, Charles Foster, Jason Phang, Horace He, Anish Thite, Noa Nabeshima, Shawn Presser, Connor Leahy
URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2101.00027

Core contribution
- Introduces The Pile: an 825 GiB English text corpus designed for training large-scale language models.
- Built from 22 diverse, high-quality subsets (many academic/professional sources) to increase training data diversity and improve cross-domain generalization.
- Publicly releases the code used to construct the dataset and provides an exploratory analysis including potentially concerning aspects for prospective users.

Key findings
- Untuned GPT-2 and GPT-3 struggle on many Pile components (e.g., academic writing).
- Models trained on The Pile significantly outperform those trained on Raw Common Crawl (Raw CC) and CC-100 across all Pile components and show improved downstream evaluation performance.
- The work underscores dataset diversity as a driver of broader knowledge and generalization in LMs.

Adoption and implementations (selected)
