- EleutherAI
  - GPT-Neo: EleutherAI/gpt-neo-125m, EleutherAI/gpt-neo-1.3B, EleutherAI/gpt-neo-2.7B (dataset: EleutherAI/pile)
  - GPT-J: EleutherAI/gpt-j-6b (dataset: EleutherAI/pile)
  - GPT-NeoX: EleutherAI/gpt-neox-20b (dataset: EleutherAI/pile)
  - Pythia suite:
    - EleutherAI/pythia-70m, EleutherAI/pythia-1b, EleutherAI/pythia-6.9b, EleutherAI/pythia-12b (dataset: EleutherAI/pile)
    - EleutherAI/pythia-12b-deduped (dataset: EleutherAI/the_pile_deduplicated)
- Cerebras-GPT
  - cerebras/Cerebras-GPT-111M, 1.3B, 2.7B, 6.7B, 13B (dataset: the_pile)

Models referencing or incorporating The Pile among broader training sources
- BSC-LT
  - BSC-LT/ALIA-40b; BSC-LT/salamandra-7b-instruct (include dataset: EleutherAI/the_pile_deduplicated among many multilingual/legal/code corpora)
- Additional ecosystems citing/related (often alongside RefinedWeb, RedPajama, Pile v2 dev, etc.)
  - Falcon series (tiiuae/falcon-7b, -40b, -180B) list the Pile paper among references (training primarily on RefinedWeb).
  - Stability AI StableLM series (e.g., stabilityai/stablelm-3b-4e1t; stablelm-2-1_6b; stablelm-2-12b; stablelm-base-alpha-7b-v2) cite related datasets and Pile v2 dev among sources; these expand beyond The Pile.

Common characteristics across implementations
- Task: text generation / causal language modeling.
- Libraries: predominantly Hugging Face Transformers; some GPT-NeoX stacks.
- Languages: largely English for Pile-trained models; some later models are multilingual and mix The Pile (or deduped variants) with other web-scale corpora.

Takeaways
- The Pile established a widely used, diverse, large-scale English corpus that improved LM performance across varied domains and influenced many open-source model releases.
- Empirical evidence shows Pile-trained models outperform comparable models trained on less diverse web corpora (Raw CC, CC-100) both on in-domain components and downstream tasks.
- The authors emphasize transparency by releasing construction code and documenting data concerns for responsible use.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2009.03300'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 227977 tokens): Title: Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU)

Authors: Dan Hendrycks; Collin Burns; Steven Basart; Andy Zou; Mantas Mazeika; Dawn Song; Jacob Steinhardt

Core idea:
- Introduces a comprehensive test to measure text models’ multitask accuracy across a broad range of academic and professional subjects.
- The benchmark spans 57 tasks, including elementary mathematics, US history, computer science, law, and more.
- To score highly, models must combine extensive world knowledge with problem-solving ability.

Key findings:
- Most recent models (at the time) perform near random chance.
- The largest GPT-3 model improves over random chance by almost 20 percentage points on average, yet remains far from expert-level accuracy across all 57 tasks.
- Performance is lopsided across subjects; models often lack calibration and “don’t know when they’re wrong.”
- Accuracy remains near random on some socially important areas, notably morality and law.

Significance:
- Provides a breadth-and-depth evaluation of academic/professional understanding.
- Useful for analyzing models across many tasks and identifying critical shortcomings.

Link:
- https://huggingface.co/papers/2009.03300 (arXiv: 2009.03300)

Adoption/implementations (examples referencing this benchmark on Hugging Face):
- Google Gemma family (base and instruction-tuned; multiple sizes and generations), e.g.:
