Authors: Karl Cobbe; Vineet Kosaraju; Mohammad Bavarian; Mark Chen; Heewoo Jun; Lukasz Kaiser; Matthias Plappert; Jerry Tworek; Jacob Hilton; Reiichiro Nakano; Christopher Hesse; John Schulman
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2110.14168

Core contributions and findings:
- Problem: Despite strong performance on many tasks, state-of-the-art language models struggle with robust multi-step mathematical reasoning.
- Dataset: Introduces GSM8K, a curated set of 8.5K high-quality, linguistically diverse grade-school math word problems designed to diagnose reasoning failures and support research.
- Baseline observation: Even the largest transformer models fail to achieve high test performance on GSM8K, despite the conceptual simplicity of the tasks.
- Method: Train a verifier model to judge the correctness of candidate solutions. At inference, generate many candidate solutions and select the one ranked highest by the verifier (sampling + verification).
- Results: Verification significantly improves performance on GSM8K and scales more effectively with increased data than a finetuning-only baseline, providing strong empirical evidence for the verifier approach.

Implementations referencing this work (selection and overview):
- Broad adoption across Google’s Gemma family and a Mistral model on Hugging Face. Models span base and instruction-tuned variants, sizes from 270M to 27B, text-only and multimodal (image-text-to-text), and include quantized GGUF releases. All use the transformers library; tasks include text generation and conversational/image-text-to-text.
