Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2109.07958'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 344676 tokens): Title: TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods
Authors: Stephanie Lin, Jacob Hilton, Owain Evans
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2109.07958

What the paper introduces
- A benchmark to measure language-model truthfulness in answering questions.
- Dataset: 817 questions across 38 categories (e.g., health, law, finance, politics).
- Questions are crafted so some humans answer falsely due to common false beliefs or misconceptions.
- Goal: assess whether models avoid imitative falsehoods learned from web text.

Key findings
- Models evaluated: GPT-3, GPT-Neo/J, GPT-2, and a T5-based model.
- Best model achieved 58% truthfulness; human performance was 94%.
- Models frequently produce false answers that mimic popular misconceptions and could deceive users.
- Larger models were generally less truthful—opposite to typical scaling trends in NLP—consistent with learning falsehoods from training data.
- Implication: Scaling alone is unlikely to improve truthfulness; targeted fine-tuning with objectives beyond web-text imitation is more promising.

