Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2502.21228'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 99191 tokens): Title: ECLeKTic: a Novel Challenge Set for Evaluation of Cross-Lingual Knowledge Transfer
Authors: Omer Goldman, Uri Shaham, Dan Malkin, Sivan Eiger, Avinatan Hassidim, Yossi Matias, Joshua Maynez, Adi Mayrav Gilady, Jason Riesa, Shruti Rijhwani, Laura Rimell, Idan Szpektor, Reut Tsarfaty, Matan Eyal
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2502.21228

What it is:
- ECLeKTic is a multilingual closed-book QA (CBQA) benchmark designed to evaluate cross-lingual knowledge transfer in a simple, black-box manner.

Motivation:
- For equitable multilingual performance, LLMs must abstract and apply knowledge across languages, not just within the language of acquisition.
- Existing literature lacks reliable, controlled measures of cross-lingual knowledge transfer.

Key idea:
- Use Wikipedia’s uneven cross-language coverage as a signal for where models must transfer knowledge across languages.
- Assumption: Wikipedia coverage approximates what knowledge is present in LLM training data.

Dataset construction:
- Identify topics with articles in one “source” language but no equivalent articles in 11 other “target” languages (12 total languages considered).
- Generate knowledge-seeking questions in the source language whose answers are supported by the source-language Wikipedia article.
- Translate these questions into the other 11 target languages where equivalent articles are absent.
- In closed-book QA, solving these translated queries requires the model to transfer knowledge from the source language (where the information likely exists in training data) to target languages (where it likely does not).

Evaluation protocol:
- Black-box, closed-book QA: models cannot retrieve external content and must rely on internalized knowledge.
- Measures whether models can answer questions in languages different from the language in which the knowledge was likely acquired.

Findings:
- Across experiments with 8 large language models, even state-of-the-art systems struggle to effectively transfer knowledge across languages.
- The same models can often answer correctly when queried in the source language but fail to generalize the knowledge to other languages.

Why it matters:
- Highlights a gap between multilingual understanding and true cross-lingual knowledge sharing.
- Provides a focused, controlled benchmark to track improvements in cross-lingual knowledge transfer.

Implementations/usage in the ecosystem:
- The paper is referenced across multiple Hugging Face model cards, notably Google’s Gemma 3 and Gemma 3n families (instruction-tuned variants spanning ~270M to 27B parameters, text-only and “any-to-any” multimodal versions, plus quantized GGUF and LiteRT variants). These tags indicate awareness/usage in the community rather than direct implementations of the dataset.

Scope summary:
- Languages: 12
- Task: Closed-book QA (no retrieval)
- Core mechanism: Wikipedia presence/absence control to force cross-lingual transfer
- Result: SOTA LLMs underperform on cross-lingual transfer relative to same-language QA


