2025-12-01 12:41:21,701 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concrete, fully worked example (with specific numbers) that demonstrates addition, multiplication, rank, determinant, and eigen-analysis for two 3×3 tensors (matrices), plus how to visualize their actions in 3D. I also give short, verifiable proofs/derivations and references, and I include reproducible Python/NumPy code so you can confirm every number on your machine.

Important note on terminology: many frameworks call any multidimensional array a “tensor.” Determinant, eigenvalues, and eigenvectors are defined for 2D square arrays (matrices). For higher-order tensors, different notions exist and aren’t covered here.

1) Concrete matrices (tensors) and why these choices help
- Let A = diag(2, 1, −2) = [[2, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, −2]]. Full rank, easy eigen-structure.
- Let B be rank-1: u v^T with u = [1, 2, −1]^T and v = [3, 0, 1]^T, so
  B = [[3, 0, 1], [6, 0, 2], [−3, 0, −1]]
  This gives a clean example of a rank-deficient matrix with a simple, verifiable eigen-structure.

2) Basic properties with numbers
- Shapes: A, B ∈ R^(3×3)
- Traces:
  tr(A) = 2 + 1 − 2 = 1
  tr(B) = 3 + 0 − 1 = 2
- Determinants:
  det(A) = 2·1·(−2) = −4
  det(B) = 0 (rank < 3)
- Ranks:
  rank(A) = 3 (det(A) ≠ 0)
  rank(B) = 1 (outer product of two nonzero vectors)
- Eigenvalues/eigenvectors
  A: eigenvalues {2, 1, −2}, eigenvectors e1, e2, e3 (standard basis).
  B (rank-1): one nonzero eigenvalue v^T u = 3·1 + 0·2 + 1·(−1) = 2 with right eigenvector u = [1, 2, −1]^T. The other two eigenvalues are 0 with eigenvectors any x satisfying v^T x = 0 (e.g., [1, 0, −3]^T and [0, 1, 0]^T).
  These facts are easy to verify: (u v^T) u = u (v^T u).

3) Addition and both kinds of multiplication
- Sum: C = A + B =
  [[5, 0, 1],
   [6, 1, 2],
   [−3, 0, −3]]
  Determinant and eigenvalues of C:
  det(C) = −12. Quick derivation: det(C − λI) = (1 − λ) det([[5 − λ, 1], [−3, −3 − λ]])
  = (1 − λ) [(5 − λ)(−3 − λ) + 3] = (1 − λ) (λ^2 − 2λ − 12)
  Eigenvalues: {1, 1 + √13, 1 − √13} ≈ {1, 4.60555, −2.60555}
  Rank(C) = 3 (because det(C) ≠ 0).
- Matrix products (composition of linear maps):
  AB = A·B =
  [[6, 0, 2],
   [6, 0, 2],
   [6, 0, 2]]
  BA = B·A =
  [[6, 0, −2],
   [12, 0, −4],
   [−6, 0, 2]]
  Both AB and BA are rank-1 (all rows of AB are identical; BA = u w^T with w = [6, 0, −2]^T). det(AB) = det(BA) = 0 (rank < 3). tr(AB) = 6 + 0 + 2 = 8; tr(BA) = 8.
  Eigenvalues:
  AB has one nonzero eigenvalue 8 with right eigenvector [1, 1, 1]^T (because AB = e r^T with e = [1,1,1], r = [6,0,2], and r^T e = 8); the other two eigenvalues are 0 with eigenvectors satisfying r^T x = 0 (e.g., [1, 0, −3]^T and [0, 1, 0]^T).
  BA has one nonzero eigenvalue 8 with right eigenvector u = [1, 2, −1]^T (since BA = u w^T and w^T u = 8); two zero eigenvalues with eigenvectors satisfying w^T x = 0 (e.g., [1, 0, 3]^T and [0, 1, 0]^T).
  These confirm standard facts: det(AB) = det(A) det(B) = 0 and AB, BA share the same nonzero eigenvalues (8), even though AB ≠ BA.
- Elementwise (Hadamard) product, if you also meant that:
  A ∘ B =
  [[6, 0, 0],
   [0, 0, 0],
   [0, 0, 2]]
  Rank(A ∘ B) = 2, det(A ∘ B) = 0, eigenvalues {6, 0, 2}.
  Caution: det(A ∘ B) generally has no simple relation to det(A) det(B).

4) Is eigen-analysis still useful when rank < size?
- Yes, but with caveats:
  For rank-deficient matrices, 0 will be an eigenvalue with algebraic multiplicity at least n − rank. You can still study nonzero eigenvalues/eigenvectors; however, non-symmetric or defective matrices may lack a full set of eigenvectors. In such cases, SVD is often more informative and numerically stable.
- For B = u v^T, the unique nonzero eigenvalue is v^T u = 2. Its single nonzero singular value is ||u||·||v|| = √6 · √10 ≈ 7.74597, which quantifies how much B stretches along its action direction. SVD exists for any matrix and directly reveals rank via the count of nonzero singular values.

5) Geometric interpretation you can visualize
- A (diag(2,1,−2)) scales x, y, z axes by 2, 1, −2. The unit sphere maps to an ellipsoid with semi-axes lengths |2|, |1|, |−2|.
- B (rank-1) maps every vector x to u (v^T x). All outputs lie on the line span(u). The unit sphere collapses to a line segment along u whose length is the singular value ||u||·||v|| ≈ 7.746.
- AB maps any x to e (r^T x); image is the line span([1,1,1]).
- BA maps any x to u (w^T x); image is the line span(u).
- A + B is full rank and combines scaling plus a rank-1 shear-like component; its eigenvalues {1, 1 ± √13} include one positive > 1 and one negative < 0, indicating stretching in one direction and flipping/compression in another.

6) Minimal, reproducible Python/NumPy check
- Run this to verify every number above and to generate 3D plots. The printed numbers should match exactly; plotting code gives the geometric picture.

Python (NumPy + Matplotlib):
import numpy as np
import numpy.linalg as LA

A = np.diag([2.0, 1.0, -2.0])
u = np.array([1.0, 2.0, -1.0])
v = np.array([3.0, 0.0, 1.0])
B = np.outer(u, v)

# Basic properties
print("A=\n", A)
print("B=\n", B)
for name, M in [("A", A), ("B", B)]:
    w, _ = LA.eig(M)
    print(f"{name}: trace={np.trace(M):.6g}, det={LA.det(M):.6g}, rank={np.linalg.matrix_rank(M)}, eigvals={np.sort_complex(w)}")

# Sums and products
C = A + B
AB = A @ B
BA = B @ A
for name, M in [("A+B", C), ("AB", AB), ("BA", BA), ("A∘B", A * B)]:
    w, _ = LA.eig(M)
    print(f"{name}:\n{M}\ntrace={np.trace(M):.6g}, det={LA.det(M):.6g}, rank={np.linalg.matrix_rank(M)}, eigvals={np.sort_complex(w)}\n")

# Singular values (SVD) — helpful for rank and geometry
for name, M in [("A", A), ("B", B), ("AB", AB), ("BA", BA), ("A+B", C)]:
    s = LA.svd(M, compute_uv=False)
    print(f"{name}: singular values={s}")

# 3D visualization: basis vectors and unit sphere mapping
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from mpl_toolkits.mplot3d import Axes3D

def plot_transform(ax, M, title):
    # basis
    I = np.eye(3)
    MI = M @ I
    colors = ['r', 'g', 'b']
    for i in range(3):
        ax.quiver(0,0,0, I[0,i], I[1,i], I[2,i], color=colors[i], linewidth=2, arrow_length_ratio=0.1)
        ax.quiver(0,0,0, MI[0,i], MI[1,i], MI[2,i], color=colors[i], linestyle='--', linewidth=2, arrow_length_ratio=0.1)
    # unit sphere to ellipsoid
    phi = np.linspace(0, np.pi, 25)
    th  = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 50)
    xs = np.outer(np.sin(phi), np.cos(th))
    ys = np.outer(np.sin(phi), np.sin(th))
    zs = np.outer(np.cos(phi), np.ones_like(th))
    X = np.stack([xs, ys, zs], axis=2).reshape(-1,3).T   # (3,N)
    Y = (M @ X).T
    ax.scatter(Y[:,0], Y[:,1], Y[:,2], s=1, alpha=0.4)
    ax.set_title(title)
    lim = 8
    ax.set_xlim([-lim, lim]); ax.set_ylim([-lim, lim]); ax.set_zlim([-lim, lim])
    ax.set_box_aspect([1,1,1])

fig = plt.figure(figsize=(12,8))
for i, (name, M) in enumerate([("A", A), ("B", B), ("A+B", C), ("AB", AB)]):
    ax = fig.add_subplot(2,2,i+1, projection='3d')
    plot_transform(ax, M, name)
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

7) Key takeaways tied to your questions
- Computing determinant and rank is straightforward and diagnostic: det ≠ 0 implies full rank; det = 0 confirms rank deficiency. In our data: det(A) = −4 → rank 3; det(B) = 0 → rank 1; det(A+B) = −12 → rank 3.
- Eigen-analysis remains meaningful for rank-deficient matrices; expect zero eigenvalues. It’s often complemented or replaced by SVD, which exists for all matrices and directly reveals rank via nonzero singular values.
- Composition AB and BA need not be equal, but they share the same nonzero eigenvalues (both had 8 here) and are both rank ≤ min(rank(A), rank(B)) = 1.
- Visualization: rank-1 maps collapse 3D space onto a line; diagonal maps scale axes; sums like A+B can be understood through their eigen-structure and by seeing how they reshape the unit sphere into an ellipsoid.

8) Sources and where each fact is verified
- Determinant basics and multiplicativity det(AB) = det(A) det(B): Wikipedia, “Determinant” (Multiplicativity), https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinant
- Rank and submultiplicativity rank(AB) ≤ min(rank(A), rank(B)): Wikipedia, “Rank (linear algebra)”, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rank_(linear_algebra)
- Eigenvalues/eigenvectors: definitions and properties; zero eigenvalues for rank-deficient matrices: Wikipedia, “Eigenvalues and eigenvectors”, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eigenvalues_and_eigenvectors
- AB and BA share nonzero eigenvalues: Wikipedia, “Eigenvalues and eigenvectors” (Property that AB and BA have the same nonzero eigenvalues), or Horn & Johnson, Matrix Analysis (2nd ed.), Theorem 1.3.20.
- Rank-1 matrix u v^T has one nonzero eigenvalue v^T u; single nonzero singular value equals ||u||·||v||: “The Matrix Cookbook” (Petersen & Pedersen), Sec. on rank-1 updates; also Wikipedia “Outer product”, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outer_product and “Rank-one matrix”, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rank_one_matrix
- SVD exists for all matrices; geometric interpretation (unit sphere to ellipsoid, singular values are semi-axes): Trefethen & Bau, Numerical Linear Algebra (1997), Ch. 2; Wikipedia, “Singular value decomposition”, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singular_value_decomposition

